# Wiring your existing SonarQube install to this bundle

You generated this bundle with `--edition none`, so it does not run SonarQube — it assumes you
already have a zip/host install of SonarQube running, and only starts the containers around it
(database is external in this mode too). These are the changes to make in *your* SonarQube's
`sonar.properties`.

## 1. Database

This bundle points the containers at the same external database you passed to `generate.py`.
Point your SonarQube at it too, if it isn't already:

```
sonar.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://${db_host}:${db_port}/${db_name}${db_url_params}
sonar.jdbc.username=${db_user}
sonar.jdbc.password=<the --db-password you passed to generate.py>
```

The database must serve TLS before SonarQube starts. SonarQube and the orchestrator use
`sslmode=verify-full`: each accepts the connection only when the certificate is valid for the
host in its own URL and the CA that signed it is one it trusts. A server with TLS off is refused.

`${db_host}` is the host the orchestrator uses, and the URL above uses it for SonarQube too. The
name has to resolve for both, and it has to be on the certificate. `host.docker.internal`
resolves for the orchestrator and often does not resolve in the JVM on the SonarQube machine.
`127.0.0.1` inside the orchestrator container is the container, not the database. When SonarQube
and the orchestrator cannot use the same name, put the name that works on the SonarQube machine
in `sonar.jdbc.url`, leave this bundle on `${db_host}`, and put both names on the certificate.

SonarQube trusts that CA through a copy of the JDK `cacerts`. The shell expands `~`;
`sonar.properties` does not, so the two properties need the absolute path printed by
`echo ~/.sonarqube/truststore.jks`:

```
mkdir -p ~/.sonarqube
cp "$JAVA_HOME/lib/security/cacerts" ~/.sonarqube/truststore.jks
chmod u+w ~/.sonarqube/truststore.jks
keytool -importcert -noprompt -alias database-ca -file <your database CA certificate> \
  -keystore ~/.sonarqube/truststore.jks -storepass changeit
```

```
sonar.web.javaAdditionalOpts=-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore=<absolute path of ~/.sonarqube/truststore.jks> -Djavax.net.ssl.trustStorePassword=changeit
sonar.ce.javaAdditionalOpts=-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore=<absolute path of ~/.sonarqube/truststore.jks> -Djavax.net.ssl.trustStorePassword=changeit
```

Copy the same CA certificate, a PEM file whose name ends in `.crt` or `.pem`, into the directory
`CUSTOM_CA_DIR` in this bundle's `.env` before `docker compose up -d`. The orchestrator reads it
from there. The certificate needs a `keyUsage` extension that includes `keyCertSign`. With
`--tls on`, that directory is `custom-ca` and already holds this bundle's CA: leave that file in
place. If `CUSTOM_CA_DIR` is unset, regenerate with `--custom-ca-dir` pointing at a directory that
contains the database CA.

